  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nAn isolated convective cell developed around 8 AM along the San \nDiego coast starting in La Jolla and eventually moving offshore near \nEncinitas. A few lightning strikes occurred near Solana \nBeach/Encinitas. Rainfall totals out of that cell ranged from 0.03 \ninches of rain to 0.36 inches between La Jolla and Encinitas. For \nthe rest of the day, dry conditions are expected west of the \nmountains but there remains slight chances of showers and isolated \nthunderstorms in the mountains and High Desert. Latest high \nresolution guidance is indicating showers that develop today and \nSaturday will see rainfall rates 0.10 inch per hour or less, with\nonly a 10 percent chance of rainfall rates exceeding that. \n\nThrough Sunday high temperatures are expected to run 5 to 10 degrees \nabove average for inland areas. Low temperatures will also run above \naverage, most noticeable in the mountains and deserts where lows \nwill be 10 to 15 degrees above average. For the inland valleys and \nmountains moderate HeatRisk is expected which means that folks who \nare sensitive heat, especially those without consistent \ncooling/hydration will be most impacted. In the deserts, major \nHeatRisk indicates anyone may see negative impacts from the heat if \nprecautions aren't taken. Make sure to stay hydrated and if possible \navoid strenuous activity during the hottest parts of the day (10 AM \nto 4 PM). High temperatures are expected to gradually decrease next \nweek, bringing temperatures back down to near or slightly below \naverage by the middle of next week. \n\nSunday through Tuesday, the ridge of high pressure will shift east \nmore over the Four Corners region. This will allow for additional \ninfluxes of moisture into the area and increase our chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms. Latest guidance doesn't show a forcing \nmechanism outside of the usual daytime heating from the sun but that \ncould change as we get closer. The current forecast keeps \nthunderstorm chances confined to the mountains, locally into the \nHigh Desert, in the afternoons each day Sunday through Tuesday. For \nSunday, the latest REFS 90th percentile hourly rainfall rate peaks \nat 0.55 inches per hour, with only 10 percent chance of exceeding \nthat.",
